review.notes:
The file has one declaration, `Erdos128.erdos_128`. Its Lean statement matches erdosproblems.com/128 exactly:
- all finite simple graphs;
- every induced subgraph on ≥ ⌊n/2⌋ vertices has more than n²/50 edges (`2|V'|+1 ≥ n`, `50e > n²`);
- conclusion `¬ G.CliqueFree 3`, i.e. G contains a triangle;
- the answer polarity is correct;
- `research open` matches the site's FALSIFIABLE (open) status, and no status issue arises.
The only defect is the docstring's missing floor (minor).

Lean checks, in /workspace/review/scratch2.lean and scratch3.lean:
- a bridge lemma: the `ncard` of the induced edge set equals the number of edges of G with both ends in the set;
- monotonicity of the induced edge count under inclusion;
- the full equivalence with the Norin–Yepremyan exactly-⌊n/2⌋ form;
- K4 satisfies the hypothesis, so it is not vacuous;
- C5 does not satisfy the hypothesis, so it is not a counterexample;
- the docstring's literal ≥ n/2 reading is refuted by C5.

Degenerate cases: for n ≤ 1, V' = ∅ satisfies the size condition, so the hypothesis fails and the implication holds vacuously. That is harmless.

Sanity search: a brute-force C search, validated on known examples, found no triangle-free graph in which every ⌊n/2⌋-set spans more than n²/50 edges, for n ≤ 13 and n = 15. The n = 14 run did not finish within 40 minutes. So the statement is not trivially refutable at small sizes.

Source conventions: Razborov (arXiv:2104.09406) normalizes odd n with fractional halves (one vertex of weight 1/2). That is a slightly stronger claim for odd n. The cited source (erdosproblems.com) and Chung–Graham use ⌊n/2⌋, which is what the Lean encodes.

I could not read the primary Erdős references ([Er93, p.344], [ErRo93], [Er97b], [EFRS94]): the Rényi archive index stops at 1989 and its PDFs are scans. Faithfulness was judged against the cited erdosproblems.com statement and the secondary literature above.

Style only, not reported: the file-level `variable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} [Fintype V]` is shadowed by the theorem's own binders and does not enter the statement.
